          "content": "To solve the equation \\((2^x - 2)(5 \\cdot 2^x + 10) = 3 \\cdot 4^x + 12\\), we will follow a step-by-step approach.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the equation for clarity:\n\\[\n(2^x - 2)(5 \\cdot 2^x + 10) = 3 \\cdot 4^x + 12\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(4^x = (2^2)^x = (2^x)^2\\). Let's substitute \\(y = 2^x\\). Then the equation becomes:\n\\[\n(y - 2)(5y + 10) = 3y^2 + 12\n\\]\n\nNext, we expand the left-hand side:\n\\[\n(y - 2)(5y + 10) = y \\cdot 5y + y \\cdot 10 - 2 \\cdot 5y - 2 \\cdot 10\n\\]\n\\[\n= 5y^2 + 10y - 10y - 20\n\\]\n\\[\n= 5y^2 - 20\n\\]\n\nSo the equation now is:\n\\[\n5y^2 - 20 = 3y^2 + 12\n\\]\n\nWe will solve this equation for \\(y\\). First, move all terms to one side to set the equation to zero:\n\\[\n5y^2 - 20 - 3y^2 - 12 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n2y^2 - 32 = 0\n\\]\n\nNext, isolate \\(y^2\\):\n\\[\n2y^2 = 32\n\\]\n\\[\ny^2 = 16\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides, we get:\n\\[\ny = 4 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y = -4\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(y = 2^x\\). Since \\(2^x\\) must be positive, we discard \\(y = -4\\). Thus:\n\\[\n2^x = 4\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(4\\) can be written as a power of \\(2\\):\n\\[\n4 = 2^2\n\\]\n\nTherefore:\n\\[\n2^x = 2^2\n\\]\n\nSince the bases are the same, we equate the exponents:\n\\[\nx = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the solution is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]"
